WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.414 --> 00:00:14.210
Now I'd like to introduce today's speaker,
Pamela Matson.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:17.800
Professor Matson is Dean of
the Standford School of Earth, Energy, and

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.640
Environmental Sciences,

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.870
as well as an internationally recognized
interdisciplinary earth scientist,

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:27.030
academic leader, and
organizational strategist.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:31.535
Her research focuses on the consequences
of agricultural intensification,

00:00:31.535 --> 00:00:36.170
land-use change, and
nitrogen deposition for ecosystem and

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:41.090
the atmosphere, and on sustainable
development issues in developing regions.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.280
Working mostly in the tropics, she and

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.720
her colleagues have identified the
negative consequences of deforestation and

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:52.120
intensive agriculture for the global and
local atmosphere, freshwater, and

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:57.600
marine systems and are working to develop
new approaches that reduced those impacts

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.680
while maintaining human livelihood and
well being.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:05.247
She also works on the vulnerability of
food and water systems to climate change.

00:01:05.247 --> 00:01:07.618
And with that,
I'd like to turn it over to Pam.

00:01:07.618 --> 00:01:09.860
Welcome.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.710
&gt;&gt; Welcome.
Thank you very much for being here.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.920
I'm glad to have a chance to
talk with you this morning.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:20.012
I'd like to talk about a framework and
an approach for pursuing sustainability,

00:01:20.012 --> 00:01:24.142
and what I'm going to do is start
with a little bit about motivation,

00:01:24.142 --> 00:01:26.825
why would we be doing this,
why do we care?

00:01:26.825 --> 00:01:30.674
And then provide a framework for
analysis and decision making,

00:01:30.674 --> 00:01:34.885
a framework that really anyone,
any leader could use.

00:01:34.885 --> 00:01:39.460
I'll talk a little bit on why this is
really hard and then add a bit about

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:44.150
the tools that we can use to help make
good decisions for sustainability.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:48.760
So first of all,
let me start with the question.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.867
There we go.

00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:58.350
Let me address the question,
what does sustainability mean?

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.321
Of course this is the term that's
used by many different people in

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:04.060
many different situations and
have different meanings.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:07.250
But there are a couple that
matter to us in this context.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:13.520
It really emerged initially
from the UN and from an ongoing

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:17.675
discussion about how to help the poorest
countries in the world develop.

00:02:17.675 --> 00:02:21.470
And in develop, well,
socially and economically.

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:24.680
Going back to
the Brundtland Report in 1989,

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:30.100
our common future basically said, look if
we're going to meet the needs of people,

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.440
we're going to be working on
that very hard right now.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:39.540
How do we do that and still not
forego opportunities for the future,

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:45.520
not compromise the future ability
of people to meet their own needs?

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:50.210
And this has been worked down very,
very hard over many decades.

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:55.210
Today, we have a new set of sustainable
development goals that the UN put

00:02:55.210 --> 00:03:00.400
together last year through an engaged
stakeholder set of discussions.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.390
And people, and organizations, and

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.270
institutions around the world
are working for those goals.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:08.600
So that's sustainable development.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:10.630
But now,
what about corporate sustainability?

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:14.165
This is another major emphasis
that has engaged lots

00:03:14.165 --> 00:03:18.676
of corporations around the world
over the last couple of decades.

00:03:18.676 --> 00:03:23.717
We think about the three legged stool, and
the triple bottom line, or the three E's

00:03:23.717 --> 00:03:28.616
to represent the fact that corporations
are now trying to make decisions that make

00:03:28.616 --> 00:03:33.660
sense economically in terms of profit,
but also socially, and environmentally.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:39.307
And in many ways, this is now
relevant to the EFC value system

00:03:39.307 --> 00:03:44.750
that many corporations
are placing on their operations.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:48.700
So why do businesses engage in this?

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.070
Lots of reasons,

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:55.560
a lot of reasons having to do with
internal business sustainability,

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:01.170
with the well-being of the company
in both the short and the long term.

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:04.860
Lots of companies have engaged for
efficiency savings,

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:08.830
it saves a lot of money by using
resources more efficiently.

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:13.630
Many are identifying growth
opportunities in new markets that

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:22.010
are related to using scarce resources more
effectively for humans and human needs.

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:24.100
There are reputation issues.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:28.824
There's certainly one way to attract,
recruit, and retain motivated

00:04:28.824 --> 00:04:33.945
employees who care about the world, and
lots of elements of risk management.

00:04:33.945 --> 00:04:37.483
But I think increasingly
corporations are buying into this,

00:04:37.483 --> 00:04:40.621
they are engaging in this
because they are concerned.

00:04:40.621 --> 00:04:45.301
They and their leaders are concerned about
the well-being of people beyond their own

00:04:45.301 --> 00:04:46.150
corporation.

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:49.990
So it's about well-being
then in both cases.

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:54.960
So I think we can,
in a sense define sustainability

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.480
with a goal of well-being
of people today and

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:05.380
in the future of companies in this
next quarter, but in the longer term.

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:06.870
And of institutions,

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:10.580
and organizations, and people here,
but also all around the world.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:13.690
That's the definition we're using for
sustainability's goal.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:18.680
So we're talking about then well-being and
then we can ask a question,

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:19.910
what does well-being mean?

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:22.850
And there's been an international
conversation about this.

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:25.490
What are the constituents of well-being?

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:28.710
And absolutely at the top of
the list are material needs.

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:32.290
How do we meet the energy, and
food, and water, and shelter, and

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:36.030
material needs of people
all around the world?

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:40.370
But there's also well-being associated
with access to health care,

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:45.120
with access to education,
with access to economic opportunities.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.380
And to the opportunities to live well and
to make profits,

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:55.520
and security, freedom of want and
fear, and so on.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:58.030
These are all on the list.

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:00.493
And when we make a list like that,

00:06:00.493 --> 00:06:03.500
we can then ask how well
are we doing in that list?

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:08.730
How well have we succeeded so
far in meeting the needs of people?

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:13.130
And there's a lot of
progress that's been made.

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:15.060
We know, for example,

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:19.740
that more people have access to more
kilocalories today than ever before.

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:21.620
So we're eating better.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:24.230
More people have access to
healthcare than ever before.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:27.080
So infant mortality rates are in decline,

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:31.380
the death of children under the age
of five, death rates have declined.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:36.190
The death rates of women in child birth
have declined, lots of indicators there.

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:41.830
And in terms of education,
the literacy rates have just skyrocketed.

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:44.970
Most children today can read.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:50.760
So lots of progress, and
maybe the best indicator for

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:54.150
that is to look at life
expectancies at first.

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:56.550
And when we think about that,
look at the graph here,

00:06:56.550 --> 00:07:00.680
it shows life expectancy has
changed from 1950 to 2010.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:04.190
In the developed countries of the world,

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:08.168
we're now living 80 years or
longer on average.

00:07:08.168 --> 00:07:12.910
But in every country around the world has

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:18.220
seen some increase in life expectancy,
so that's a very good signal.

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:23.210
But you can also see in this graph the
real problem, and that is a huge disparity

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:26.230
between people living in the wealthiest
countries of the world and

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:29.410
people living in the very
poor parts of the world.

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:33.590
And that disparity shows up in
a number of different areas.

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:36.670
We have today a billion
people who can't read.

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:38.770
They're mostly adult women.

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:43.773
We have a billion without adequate
shelter or access to safe water,

00:07:43.773 --> 00:07:47.207
or to sanitation, or
modern forms of energy.

00:07:47.207 --> 00:07:51.812
Almost a billion people go to
bed hungry a lot of times.

00:07:51.812 --> 00:07:55.560
And that's today, so
a huge challenge there.

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:59.010
But the planet is still growing,
our population is still growing.

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:02.040
So let's take a quick look at that
to get a sense for the issue.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:06.866
So homo sapiens have been on the planet
for a couple hundred thousand years.

00:08:06.866 --> 00:08:11.770
But just over the last 7, or 8,
9,000 years we've gotten to grow.

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:15.521
And it's because of at first,
because of settled agriculture,

00:08:15.521 --> 00:08:21.460
because of the domestication of animals,
because people were living in settlements.

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:22.090
And so

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:27.390
by the time of Christ we had something
like 300 million people on the planet.

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:34.800
We had grown, we continue to
grow very slowly with more and

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:41.880
more people living in areas and run into
some problems like the Black Plague.

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:45.250
But in the 1800 everything changed.

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:50.990
Our population growth absolutely
took off incredibly quickly,

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:54.129
and in fact, we are still growing.

00:08:54.129 --> 00:08:57.130
But we're not growing
exponentially anymore.

00:08:57.130 --> 00:09:01.701
We are quite clearly going to
level off at a global population

00:09:01.701 --> 00:09:04.397
level of 11 billion people or so.

00:09:04.397 --> 00:09:09.590
Probably towards the end of this
century or the early part of the next.

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:13.230
That's a lot more people on
the planet that we don't have today,

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:15.850
whose needs are going to have to be met.

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:19.210
Whose well-being is a concern for
all of us.

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:25.170
Now that population is not distributed
evenly all around the planet,

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:28.100
and the growth and
change in it is not distributed evenly.

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:33.190
So, in this figure you can see
the red line towards the bottom

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:36.300
is the population in the developed
countries of the world.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:39.290
You can see they've leveled off,
they're not growing.

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:45.060
The green line shows
the population changes in the less

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:50.990
developed countries of the world, like in
this case China, and India, and Indonesia.

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:54.716
Growing very rapidly at first,
now within a decade, or two, or

00:09:54.716 --> 00:09:56.105
three of leveling off.

00:09:56.105 --> 00:10:00.873
And then in the bottom purple line
you see the continued increase in

00:10:00.873 --> 00:10:06.430
population growth in, what are the very
least developed areas of the world.

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:09.919
The countries of Sub-Saharan Africa for
example.

00:10:09.919 --> 00:10:15.536
So we can look at that, and
see where the changes are occurring,

00:10:15.536 --> 00:10:20.321
and where people will need
the most new resources, and

00:10:20.321 --> 00:10:23.480
will need to work for well-being.

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:26.224
The other point about this slide is that,
or

00:10:26.224 --> 00:10:31.042
not in the slide actually is that lot of
these people are living in cities now,

00:10:31.042 --> 00:10:36.486
more than 50% today, probably more than
70% to 80% by the end of the century.

00:10:36.486 --> 00:10:40.481
And even as population is slowing down and
stopping,

00:10:40.481 --> 00:10:46.716
population growth is stopping, consumption
is continuing, it's increasing.

00:10:46.716 --> 00:10:51.644
And that is good because many people right
now don't have access to enough things

00:10:51.644 --> 00:10:53.100
in the world.

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:58.635
And, many people around the world
are moving into the middle class.

00:10:58.635 --> 00:11:00.480
Though consumption will increase as well.

00:11:02.830 --> 00:11:04.940
So you look at those trends, and

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:10.449
I hope you have seen huge opportunities
in this, but also huge challenges.

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:14.150
How are we going to meet
the needs of that growing, and

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:15.910
more consuming human population?

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:21.211
How are we going to do it in a way
that can be sustained over decades and

00:11:21.211 --> 00:11:22.240
centuries?

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:28.000
And what does this mean for
all of the businesses in which we work?

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:32.730
How do we work towards into generation
of well-being given the situation.

00:11:34.078 --> 00:11:39.780
So, I'm going to show you right now
a framework that was developed and

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:42.680
published in this book called
Pursuing Sustainability.

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:46.980
It's just a primer, and this framework
was intended for any leader.

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:49.400
Not just business leaders,

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:54.140
as the way of thinking about
the pursuit of sustainability.

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:58.260
At the top of the framework
we think about our goal,

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:00.942
the goal of intergenerational well-being.

00:12:00.942 --> 00:12:05.610
And well-being of people, here and
around the world today and in the future.

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:07.665
So how do we meet that goal.

00:12:07.665 --> 00:12:10.497
Well through a whole
bunch of production and

00:12:10.497 --> 00:12:13.950
consumption activities
that we're all engaged in.

00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:17.530
We're creating the goods and
services and trying to provide them for

00:12:17.530 --> 00:12:19.360
people to consume.

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:22.320
We are the actors and agents doing that.

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:26.700
And there's lots and lots of ways
that we can do those things better or

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:31.880
worse that will have an impact on long
term intergenerational well-being.

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:34.930
We could spend hours talking
through these things,

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:38.700
but what we're going to do
right now is move down to

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:43.340
the underlying assets that control
what we can produce and consume.

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:47.750
We’re going to talk about five
capital assets that ultimately

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:51.150
determine human well-being.

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:55.050
So, let’s start at the top,
Natural Capital, this is an asset,

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:58.710
these are the people of the world,
people from different places,

00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:04.320
it’s the numbers of people,
their demographics, their health status,

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:08.410
their level of education and knowledge
accumulation, all of the things that

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:13.340
allow people, individual people,
to be actors and decision makers.

00:13:15.010 --> 00:13:16.410
We have Social Capital.

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:19.940
Those are our rules and
regulations, the norms and

00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:23.360
institutions, the networks that we use.

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:29.850
The strategies including financing
strategies that allow us to move and

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:31.740
make decisions for sustainability.

00:13:32.770 --> 00:13:35.860
Over on the other side of the graph
we have Manufactured Capital.

00:13:35.860 --> 00:13:38.670
This is everything we build,
everything we make.

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:44.200
It's our buildings, our factories,
the stuff we make inside of the factories,

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:48.530
the new technology and
tools, it's our roads, and

00:13:48.530 --> 00:13:51.200
our airports, and
everything else that the humans make.

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:55.500
Over on the right we
have Knowledge Capital.

00:13:55.500 --> 00:13:59.500
This is the kind of new knowledge
that's created in places like

00:13:59.500 --> 00:14:04.500
Stanford University, other universities,
research centers of companies and

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:05.800
of governments.

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:09.840
And it's also the knowledge that is
a pain through practice in experience

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:12.350
in the field or in the factory.

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:16.835
Crucial to have that knowledge as we move
ahead in a transition to sustainability.

00:14:16.835 --> 00:14:19.842
And then finally at the bottom,
is Natural Capital.

00:14:19.842 --> 00:14:23.130
This is basically our life support system.

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:26.243
It's all of the resources that we call on,

00:14:26.243 --> 00:14:31.786
in order to power that manufactured
capital and to meet the needs of people.

00:14:31.786 --> 00:14:35.777
It's the place,
it's the environment in which we live,

00:14:35.777 --> 00:14:38.474
our atmosphere, our climate system.

00:14:38.474 --> 00:14:43.058
And the species and the ecosystems on
land and in the ocean, as it provides so

00:14:43.058 --> 00:14:45.000
many of the things that we need.

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:46.800
So that's natural capital.

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:53.400
Those five capital assets underlie our
ability to meet our goal of well-being.

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:58.894
And the bottom line is if they go down,
if we lose them, or if they're declining.

00:14:58.894 --> 00:15:00.988
Then we're in trouble,

00:15:00.988 --> 00:15:05.983
if our goal is to worry about
the future as well as right now.

00:15:05.983 --> 00:15:10.099
So, I would in a normal kind
of classroom environment,

00:15:10.099 --> 00:15:14.300
I'd ask you right now,
which of these capitals.

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:19.820
Capital assets do you think is under
most stress, which ones are declining.

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:23.190
And some of you would say social capital.

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:28.700
You would say without the rule of law,
we can't make any progress.

00:15:28.700 --> 00:15:29.900
And you'd be right.

00:15:29.900 --> 00:15:33.110
Others might say it's manufacture capital.

00:15:33.110 --> 00:15:38.759
We don't have the right infrastructure to
allow these new technologies to take off,

00:15:38.759 --> 00:15:40.804
and you'd be right there too.

00:15:40.804 --> 00:15:47.230
But if you're thinking globally,
the answer is natural capital.

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:49.790
Globally, there is no doubt that

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:54.070
our whole range of natural
capital assets are in decline.

00:15:54.070 --> 00:15:56.070
And it's not hard to see why.

00:15:56.070 --> 00:16:00.180
If you go back to this
population growth graph.

00:16:00.180 --> 00:16:02.610
And you look at that dramatic increase.

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:07.571
In 1800, we had 1 billion people.

00:16:07.571 --> 00:16:10.281
By 1924, we had 2 billion people.

00:16:10.281 --> 00:16:12.927
By the 1950, we had 3 billion people.

00:16:12.927 --> 00:16:15.727
Now, we have 7.4 billion and
we're going to 11.

00:16:15.727 --> 00:16:19.378
If you look at that graph and
how quickly that happened,

00:16:19.378 --> 00:16:24.284
you can kind of understand why our life
support system had been in distress.

00:16:24.284 --> 00:16:29.692
I mean, we've been in a mad dash
to meet the need of people and

00:16:29.692 --> 00:16:32.464
we've done that pretty well.

00:16:32.464 --> 00:16:36.149
We've met need for
energy by using biomass and

00:16:36.149 --> 00:16:41.190
most especially fossil fuels,
coal, oil and gas.

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:46.600
We've met the needs of people for
food through some

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:51.300
wonderful new technologies that increased
yields, also through virgin lands,

00:16:51.300 --> 00:16:53.800
natural lands, agriculture and

00:16:53.800 --> 00:16:58.140
we kept food pace, the food production
had pace with human population growth.

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:05.138
We've met the needs of people for water
for a whole range in different uses by

00:17:05.138 --> 00:17:10.780
building reservoirs, by pumping aqua
first, by using water in rivers and

00:17:10.780 --> 00:17:14.919
we've succeeded in all of these things and
many more.

00:17:14.919 --> 00:17:19.774
However, many of those efforts have had
unintended negative consequences and

00:17:19.774 --> 00:17:24.280
simply we didn't take time to worry
about the impact on natural capital.

00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:30.728
So burning of fossil fuels, absolutely
crucial for meeting energy needs and

00:17:30.728 --> 00:17:37.289
yet is leading to very high emissions
of air pollutants and greenhouse gases.

00:17:37.289 --> 00:17:41.125
Likewise, greenhouse gases,
methane, nitrous oxide,

00:17:41.125 --> 00:17:46.033
carbon dioxide coming from agriculture and
those together add up to a global

00:17:46.033 --> 00:17:49.502
change in temperature that
we're seeing right now.

00:17:49.502 --> 00:17:51.943
There's absolutely no doubt of that and

00:17:51.943 --> 00:17:56.120
that temperature change is
having a range of impacts.

00:17:56.120 --> 00:18:00.160
Things like very heavy precipitation
events and flooding or

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:04.960
extended droughts, or sea level rise,
or increased storminess.

00:18:04.960 --> 00:18:08.484
That ultimately affect our ability
to meet the needs of people.

00:18:08.484 --> 00:18:14.027
So, the affect are sustainability goals
and climate isn't the only thing.

00:18:14.027 --> 00:18:20.918
There's a whole, long list of ways that
our natural capital is being degraded.

00:18:20.918 --> 00:18:25.616
And again, we could spend a lot
of time focusing on the different

00:18:25.616 --> 00:18:29.444
items in this list, but
I'm going to move on to say,

00:18:29.444 --> 00:18:33.794
the real challenge here is to
change that downward arrow for

00:18:33.794 --> 00:18:39.362
natural capital to an upward arrow while
still meeting the needs of people,

00:18:39.362 --> 00:18:43.886
while still paid attention to
the well-being of our people in

00:18:43.886 --> 00:18:49.019
organizations today as well as in
the future and I think we can do that.

00:18:49.019 --> 00:18:53.783
There are fantastic examples right now
of companies engaging in doing exactly

00:18:53.783 --> 00:18:56.534
that and
we'll come back to that in a minute.

00:18:56.534 --> 00:19:01.349
I want to tell you a quick story about how
our efforts to meet the needs of people

00:19:01.349 --> 00:19:03.759
have had unintended consequences and

00:19:03.759 --> 00:19:08.822
then we'll go to some stories of where
things are going in the right direction.

00:19:08.822 --> 00:19:15.455
So green revolution, the green revolution
took place in the 1950s through the 1990s.

00:19:15.455 --> 00:19:20.498
Really, launched by a group of academic
scientists as well as nongovernmental

00:19:20.498 --> 00:19:25.694
organizations who recognized that human
population was growing very quickly and

00:19:25.694 --> 00:19:30.510
there was no way we could meet the food
needs of those people without changing

00:19:30.510 --> 00:19:31.891
the way we do things.

00:19:31.891 --> 00:19:36.878
So they created, they invested in
creating a number of new technologies.

00:19:36.878 --> 00:19:40.893
Improved genetic materials
of their cereal crops.

00:19:40.893 --> 00:19:45.138
New ways to carry out irrigation,
new kinds of industrial fertilizers,

00:19:45.138 --> 00:19:47.405
new pesticides, new mechanization.

00:19:47.405 --> 00:19:53.201
And as you would recognize, a lot of those
new technologies launched companies or

00:19:53.201 --> 00:19:56.821
rather companies launch
those new technologies.

00:19:56.821 --> 00:19:59.423
Huge advancement there.

00:19:59.423 --> 00:20:03.219
Of course, the new technologies themselves
are weren't going to be enough.

00:20:03.219 --> 00:20:09.491
The people in the field have to know how
to use them, so both the companies and

00:20:09.491 --> 00:20:15.383
the nongovernmental organizations
began to invest in human capital.

00:20:15.383 --> 00:20:21.207
Training farmers in making sure that
they have the education needed in order

00:20:21.207 --> 00:20:27.399
to employ those technologies and that
whole system yielded a fantastic result.

00:20:27.399 --> 00:20:31.640
We were able to keep food production
at pace with that very rapid

00:20:31.640 --> 00:20:33.494
human population growth.

00:20:33.494 --> 00:20:38.496
Rally wonderful, but one problem
was that nobody was really paying

00:20:38.496 --> 00:20:42.980
a whole lot of attention to
the draw down in natural capital.

00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:49.312
The soil resources, the effect
of fertilizers on water systems,

00:20:49.312 --> 00:20:54.087
the use of water for irrigation and
over pumping of aquifers.

00:20:54.087 --> 00:20:58.209
Air pollution problems,
green house gas emissions and on and on.

00:20:58.209 --> 00:21:03.932
Again, changes that affect our ability
to meet the needs of people for

00:21:03.932 --> 00:21:05.590
food in the future.

00:21:05.590 --> 00:21:07.522
So, that went down.

00:21:07.522 --> 00:21:11.158
There was also a bit of a drop
in social and natural capital in

00:21:11.158 --> 00:21:16.050
some places as whole communities,
especially indigenous groups, lost out.

00:21:16.050 --> 00:21:19.086
Lost their land and
lost their ability to play.

00:21:19.086 --> 00:21:23.550
Well, since then, all of our efforts
within the last couple of decades have

00:21:23.550 --> 00:21:25.444
been trying to reimburse that.

00:21:25.444 --> 00:21:27.290
How can we do this differently?

00:21:27.290 --> 00:21:31.624
How can we still grow enough food,
engage enough companies and

00:21:31.624 --> 00:21:35.713
engage enough individuals to
meet the needs of people, but

00:21:35.713 --> 00:21:41.910
without those negative unintended
consequences and there are great stories.

00:21:41.910 --> 00:21:44.500
I'm going to just share one story and

00:21:44.500 --> 00:21:48.510
that is the story of the sustainable
harvest coffee importers,

00:21:48.510 --> 00:21:53.080
because I think it illustrates paying
attention to all of these assets.

00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:58.210
So David Griswold, the founder
in the 1980s soon after college,

00:21:58.210 --> 00:22:03.240
I guess volunteered by working with
poor coffee farmers in Mexico and

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:06.110
he began to see how
difficult their lives were.

00:22:06.110 --> 00:22:09.917
How challenging it was despite
all their hard work for

00:22:09.917 --> 00:22:15.331
their well-being to increase and
he decided later to create a business that

00:22:15.331 --> 00:22:20.596
would help them improve their wellbeing,
but would also be profitable.

00:22:20.596 --> 00:22:22.700
So, what did he worry about?

00:22:22.700 --> 00:22:26.283
He worried about finding
that knowledge capital and

00:22:26.283 --> 00:22:31.904
creating that new knowledge that they
could employ in technologies in the field,

00:22:31.904 --> 00:22:36.871
their manufacture capital that would
allow farmers to get more value for

00:22:36.871 --> 00:22:39.248
the crops that they were growing.

00:22:39.248 --> 00:22:43.560
That would allow them to reduce
the cost of fertilizers and

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:49.730
add more of the resources they had at
hand including organic fertilizers.

00:22:49.730 --> 00:22:52.480
That would reduce the environmental
consequences of what they do.

00:22:53.900 --> 00:22:55.040
He recognized again,

00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:58.582
that you can have new technology that
people need to know how to use it.

00:22:58.582 --> 00:23:03.025
Farmers were engaged,
were involved in learning and training and

00:23:03.025 --> 00:23:05.895
using and improving those technologies.

00:23:05.895 --> 00:23:11.949
So he worked with the capital asset
of human capital and he made sure and

00:23:11.949 --> 00:23:17.697
his teams made sure that the natural
capital wasn't declining and

00:23:17.697 --> 00:23:23.450
actually was improving,
because of these processes.

00:23:23.450 --> 00:23:28.408
He also focused on supply chain
dynamics and increased the transparency

00:23:28.408 --> 00:23:33.526
of the supply chain, so that those
poor farmers actually understood where

00:23:33.526 --> 00:23:38.319
their coffee was going and who was
making money on it and built a trust in

00:23:38.319 --> 00:23:43.380
a long term consistency in the supply
chain that pays that back over time.

00:23:43.380 --> 00:23:46.041
Ultimately, producing a lot of coffee.

00:23:46.041 --> 00:23:51.342
Increasing the well-being of the people
there and creating a profitable company.

00:23:51.342 --> 00:23:54.990
The story is not over and
it could go on and on,

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:59.420
but it gives you an idea of
what one corporation can do.

00:24:02.180 --> 00:24:03.570
So I think all the challenge for

00:24:03.570 --> 00:24:08.090
all of us is to think about how
me manage all of these assets and

00:24:08.090 --> 00:24:11.470
their interactions in ways that
promote intergenerational well-being.

00:24:11.470 --> 00:24:14.537
And again,
what I would do in classroom situation,

00:24:14.537 --> 00:24:17.046
if you were all sitting
here with us today,

00:24:17.046 --> 00:24:21.878
is ask you to apply the framework to your
challenge, to your business challenge.

00:24:21.878 --> 00:24:25.830
In a way,
it's a way of thinking about the problem.

00:24:25.830 --> 00:24:30.339
It's a checklist to remind us not
to forget really important assets

00:24:30.339 --> 00:24:31.768
as we make our plans.

00:24:31.768 --> 00:24:35.020
But you can take that home and
do it later.

00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:41.411
So I have to be truthful in saying,
this is not as easy as it sounds.

00:24:41.411 --> 00:24:44.007
This is a really hard problem.

00:24:44.007 --> 00:24:47.130
And it's because these systems are very,
very complex.

00:24:48.560 --> 00:24:57.330
They are not simple, linear relationships
among the different components of them.

00:24:57.330 --> 00:25:04.450
So just briefly,
let me say what a system is.

00:25:04.450 --> 00:25:07.121
So we talked about complex systems.

00:25:07.121 --> 00:25:12.341
A system, as defined by a huge field
of inquiry, of systems thinking,

00:25:12.341 --> 00:25:17.040
systems dynamics, systems engineering,
systems analysis,

00:25:17.040 --> 00:25:22.696
systems ecology, are all focused on
understanding these complex systems of

00:25:22.696 --> 00:25:29.490
interconnected elements that are organized
in a way that achieves something.

00:25:29.490 --> 00:25:32.470
So the boat in the picture is a system,
but

00:25:33.490 --> 00:25:38.400
the lake and the shoreline and the rescue

00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:43.800
organizations that the boat is surrounded
by could also be considered the system.

00:25:43.800 --> 00:25:46.584
Likewise, your factory is a system,

00:25:46.584 --> 00:25:51.282
but your business contains supply
chain and other elements that

00:25:51.282 --> 00:25:56.171
are probably a better way to think
about your business as a system.

00:25:56.171 --> 00:25:59.472
And for sustainability,
we have to broaden these boundaries.

00:25:59.472 --> 00:26:04.340
So that we include the environment and
the well-being of people as well.

00:26:05.400 --> 00:26:10.779
Okay, but systems dynamics has
focused a lot on the challenges

00:26:10.779 --> 00:26:15.053
of understanding and
managing complex systems.

00:26:15.053 --> 00:26:19.163
And there's a whole list of
characteristics that make a system,

00:26:19.163 --> 00:26:23.285
whether it's your business system or
anything else difficult.

00:26:23.285 --> 00:26:28.633
And I'm going to pick out just a couple of
these to illustrate a way of thinking and

00:26:28.633 --> 00:26:32.292
a way of analyzing our decisions and
interventions.

00:26:32.292 --> 00:26:36.721
So that we are trying to assure that
they're not resulting in negative,

00:26:36.721 --> 00:26:39.300
unintended consequences.

00:26:39.300 --> 00:26:42.491
So let me start with positive and
negative feedbacks.

00:26:42.491 --> 00:26:47.100
I'm going to just jump into
an example here to illustrate this.

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:51.900
This is a positive feedback cycle that
plays out in many different areas and many

00:26:51.900 --> 00:26:57.940
different organizations would approach
sustainability of water use in this way.

00:26:57.940 --> 00:27:01.800
So we have an effort going
on at conservation of water.

00:27:01.800 --> 00:27:02.560
Why are we doing this?

00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:07.162
Because this community realized that
it was using too much water and

00:27:07.162 --> 00:27:09.917
their water resources were going down.

00:27:09.917 --> 00:27:13.078
And they were worried about where they
were going to get it in the future.

00:27:13.078 --> 00:27:16.561
So they started to invest in
conservation of water, and

00:27:16.561 --> 00:27:21.130
they saw great results,
great water efficiency results.

00:27:21.130 --> 00:27:25.099
That led to a recognition and
an awareness of the positive

00:27:25.099 --> 00:27:29.401
results which then continued to
encourage public support for

00:27:29.401 --> 00:27:33.966
that water conservation,
which then led to more conservation.

00:27:33.966 --> 00:27:35.556
So that's the positive feedback cycle.

00:27:35.556 --> 00:27:39.674
That's sometimes called a virtuous cycle,

00:27:39.674 --> 00:27:44.460
sometimes called a reinforcing
feedback process.

00:27:44.460 --> 00:27:49.382
And over on the right, what resulted was
that because of the water efficiency

00:27:49.382 --> 00:27:52.875
results, they took less
total water from the rivers.

00:27:52.875 --> 00:27:57.535
And they had more stream flows that
they could count on for the long term.

00:27:57.535 --> 00:28:01.502
So that's the great success story there,
seemed to work.

00:28:01.502 --> 00:28:02.942
But one problem for

00:28:02.942 --> 00:28:08.711
them is that their boundary around
their system wasn't quite wide enough.

00:28:08.711 --> 00:28:13.452
What happens in the situation is that
because there was less total water

00:28:13.452 --> 00:28:17.719
being taken from the rivers,
there was more available water for

00:28:17.719 --> 00:28:21.293
new business and
new homes to come in and they came in.

00:28:21.293 --> 00:28:25.241
They came into the region, and
that increased total water demand.

00:28:25.241 --> 00:28:29.816
And that increased the amount
of water taken from rivers and

00:28:29.816 --> 00:28:31.932
led to lower stream flows.

00:28:31.932 --> 00:28:37.005
So that was a negative feedback,
a balancing feedback cycle

00:28:37.005 --> 00:28:41.980
that unfortunately led to that
original effort to increase

00:28:41.980 --> 00:28:46.730
sustainability of water
use to be not successful.

00:28:46.730 --> 00:28:49.530
These kinds of things happen everywhere.

00:28:49.530 --> 00:28:53.162
Another quick example of that,
this an example,

00:28:53.162 --> 00:28:57.750
say that we're in a community where
they're a very underprivileged community.

00:28:57.750 --> 00:29:02.660
Where a lot of people don't have
access to services, and to schools,

00:29:02.660 --> 00:29:05.150
and playgrounds, and
hospitals, and so forth.

00:29:05.150 --> 00:29:10.580
The leaders in this area, the government,
the non-governmental organizations, and

00:29:10.580 --> 00:29:15.750
corporations decided to invest in
improving infrastructure and services.

00:29:17.170 --> 00:29:20.400
That led to an increase in the well-being
of the community residents,

00:29:20.400 --> 00:29:22.600
so exactly what they wanted to do.

00:29:22.600 --> 00:29:26.980
And that led to more support,
a success story, and

00:29:26.980 --> 00:29:30.380
it continued government, NGO,
and corporate investment.

00:29:30.380 --> 00:29:33.668
Another positive feedback cycle,

00:29:33.668 --> 00:29:38.602
another reinforcing one,
another virtuous cycle.

00:29:38.602 --> 00:29:43.611
But when others outside that region,
who were still underprivileged,

00:29:43.611 --> 00:29:49.358
found out about it, they saw the signs of
well-being and they moved to the region so

00:29:49.358 --> 00:29:53.981
that they could have access to
that infrastructure and services.

00:29:53.981 --> 00:29:58.894
And what happened then is that once again,
that community did not have enough

00:29:58.894 --> 00:30:02.842
infrastructure and
services to meet the needs of the people.

00:30:02.842 --> 00:30:08.561
So the positive feedback was pushed
back by the negative feedback.

00:30:08.561 --> 00:30:13.642
These kinds of feedback cycles
happen everywhere, and they're so

00:30:13.642 --> 00:30:19.290
common that they fall into an archetype
that's called fixes that fail.

00:30:19.290 --> 00:30:24.180
You have a problem, you put a fix in
place, you solve the problem for a while,

00:30:24.180 --> 00:30:26.980
but then you get unintended consequences.

00:30:28.020 --> 00:30:32.070
And part of the problem is
the scale at you you're looking,

00:30:32.070 --> 00:30:35.000
the boundaries that you have around
your system of are too small.

00:30:36.020 --> 00:30:40.660
And there are a lot of
these kinds of feedback

00:30:40.660 --> 00:30:45.890
cycles that happen over and
over again in municipalities,

00:30:45.890 --> 00:30:52.070
in communities of all kinds, in companies,
in all kinds of organizations.

00:30:52.070 --> 00:30:56.220
The good thing about it is they happen so
often that there's a list of them now.

00:30:56.220 --> 00:31:00.820
There's ten different archetypes for
these feedbacks that will get in the way

00:31:00.820 --> 00:31:04.790
of efforts to shift towards
a sustainability goal.

00:31:05.930 --> 00:31:10.260
And you can manage for
those kinds of feedback situations,

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:12.300
if you're aware of them.

00:31:12.300 --> 00:31:16.561
This is an area that we could
go into a lot more deeply,

00:31:16.561 --> 00:31:21.317
but I want to move on very quickly
to a couple of other issues.

00:31:21.317 --> 00:31:26.043
Another challenge that we run into as
we're trying to manage these complex

00:31:26.043 --> 00:31:30.770
systems for sustainability is that
there's all kinds of connections that

00:31:30.770 --> 00:31:36.210
takes place across space and time,
and there's a lot of invisibilities.

00:31:36.210 --> 00:31:42.179
There's a lot of situations where what I
do as a citizen is having impacts far,

00:31:42.179 --> 00:31:46.536
far away and I don't even realize it,
so invisibility.

00:31:46.536 --> 00:31:49.690
So we sometimes get in trouble
because of that, when cause and

00:31:49.690 --> 00:31:54.072
effect are distant in time or in space,

00:31:54.072 --> 00:31:58.720
and when solutions require actions
at number of different scales.

00:31:59.780 --> 00:32:04.680
Let me give you a quick example
of this fascinating example, but

00:32:04.680 --> 00:32:07.270
has some solutions embedded now.

00:32:07.270 --> 00:32:10.059
And that is an example around palm oil.

00:32:10.059 --> 00:32:14.290
So I'm drawing here on work
by one of my colleagues here,

00:32:14.290 --> 00:32:18.267
Jim Leape, at Woods Institute for
the Environment.

00:32:18.267 --> 00:32:23.920
So palm oil is an ingredient in half
of the products in the grocery store.

00:32:23.920 --> 00:32:29.740
We all use it all the time in many
different kinds of purchases that we make.

00:32:31.060 --> 00:32:33.590
If you go through the long supply chain

00:32:33.590 --> 00:32:37.530
you can ask the question where
does that palm oil originate from?

00:32:37.530 --> 00:32:40.780
What is needed at the base of
the supply chain for that?

00:32:40.780 --> 00:32:45.075
And the answer is, it comes from palm
oil plants from palm plantation.

00:32:45.075 --> 00:32:51.110
50% of the palm plantations in
the world around that are in Indonesia.

00:32:51.110 --> 00:32:54.500
There's maybe another third in Malaysia,
or

00:32:54.500 --> 00:32:57.800
at least the palm oil is coming
from Malaysian plantations.

00:32:57.800 --> 00:33:02.860
And palm oil plantations are also
expanding now into parts of

00:33:02.860 --> 00:33:06.500
Central and Eastern Africa and
Latin America.

00:33:07.600 --> 00:33:08.660
So that's fine.

00:33:08.660 --> 00:33:14.810
That's an agricultural crop that is needed
but there are a couple problems with it.

00:33:14.810 --> 00:33:19.912
And one is that most of the palm
plantations were originally and

00:33:19.912 --> 00:33:25.205
quite recently tropical forests
often with very heavy peak soils

00:33:25.205 --> 00:33:30.209
that have a lot of carbon in it and
as the forests are burned to be

00:33:30.209 --> 00:33:36.104
converted into plantation,
they emit huge amounts of air pollutants.

00:33:36.104 --> 00:33:37.492
And so you see pictures,

00:33:37.492 --> 00:33:41.994
I'm sure you've seen pictures of people
living in downwind cities with masks on

00:33:41.994 --> 00:33:47.010
because they're suffering from air
pollution from the biomass burning.

00:33:47.010 --> 00:33:49.285
But they also emit a lot
of greenhouse gases.

00:33:50.360 --> 00:33:56.580
And then of concern to many communities is
that they are a threat to biodiversity.

00:33:57.750 --> 00:33:59.420
There's a threat of extinction.

00:33:59.420 --> 00:34:04.450
This is a graphic from the World
Wildlife Fund focusing on a species

00:34:04.450 --> 00:34:09.630
that is under huge stress because of
deforestation related to palm oil.

00:34:11.160 --> 00:34:14.730
So the question that is,
what do we do about that?

00:34:14.730 --> 00:34:20.460
How do we meet the needs for palm oil
without those negative consequences?

00:34:20.460 --> 00:34:29.290
That question was addressed; it has been
a big problem that has been approached.

00:34:29.290 --> 00:34:35.750
Very slowly over time, but it's growing
right now in terms of the action.

00:34:35.750 --> 00:34:40.175
So going back to 2001,
a small Swiss retailer,

00:34:40.175 --> 00:34:44.658
[INAUDIBLE] got together with a couple
of conservation organizations,

00:34:44.658 --> 00:34:49.780
non-government organizations and

00:34:49.780 --> 00:34:55.390
then they brought stakeholders together
to create a certification system.

00:34:55.390 --> 00:35:01.420
They developed a round table on palm oil,
and they created together

00:35:01.420 --> 00:35:05.900
a set of standards that would allow for
palm oil to be certified sustainable.

00:35:05.900 --> 00:35:09.210
So that was an important first step,
seemed to make sense.

00:35:09.210 --> 00:35:11.570
The next step was that they then and

00:35:11.570 --> 00:35:16.000
other NGOs came in at this point
tried to engage some of the biggest

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:20.590
corporations in the world who sourced
palm oil from palm plantations.

00:35:21.670 --> 00:35:25.350
And the first one to be brought in was
Unilever, and not surprisingly Unilever

00:35:25.350 --> 00:35:30.420
has a long record of social
responsibility, and they agreed

00:35:30.420 --> 00:35:35.350
that they wanted to use certified palm
oil and not have deforestation occurring.

00:35:35.350 --> 00:35:38.390
And then over time many, many,
other players have come in,

00:35:38.390 --> 00:35:44.260
there's a true partnership among these
corporations as they seek to source

00:35:44.260 --> 00:35:50.430
palm oil from systems where
deforestation was not a factor.

00:35:50.430 --> 00:35:53.810
So that story goes on too, but
it's a nice example of how partnership

00:35:53.810 --> 00:35:57.600
can come together to work
towards sustainability.

00:35:59.090 --> 00:36:04.110
I'm going to move on passed
this next characteristic,

00:36:04.110 --> 00:36:08.850
although it's a fascinating one and
wrap this up.

00:36:10.200 --> 00:36:14.490
So we have a lot of complexity out
there as we are trying to work

00:36:14.490 --> 00:36:17.881
towards long-term
intergenerational human wellbeing.

00:36:18.920 --> 00:36:24.430
And it would be nice if we could say
there's one great set of models or

00:36:24.430 --> 00:36:26.070
simulation models or whatever.

00:36:26.070 --> 00:36:30.560
That all you have to do is put
in an intervention and the model

00:36:30.560 --> 00:36:34.720
will tell you whether you're going to have
unintended negative consequences or not.

00:36:34.720 --> 00:36:38.980
The model will tell you if it's
a smart move to make or not.

00:36:38.980 --> 00:36:43.024
Unfortunately, no such
integrated systems model exists.

00:36:43.024 --> 00:36:47.370
And if there were, we probably
wouldn't believe it, too complex.

00:36:47.370 --> 00:36:49.543
But there are lots of
tool that we can use.

00:36:49.543 --> 00:36:51.390
This is a list of some of them.

00:36:52.410 --> 00:36:56.840
Many of these are use right now by
corporations that are trying to make sure

00:36:56.840 --> 00:37:01.905
that they have sustainable supply chain
and many more could be used in the future.

00:37:02.915 --> 00:37:07.905
In our discussions and courses here we
can dive into these with some detail and

00:37:07.905 --> 00:37:12.705
give examples of how these different tools
are actually used to help decision makers

00:37:12.705 --> 00:37:15.035
make good decisions for sustainability.

00:37:15.035 --> 00:37:18.056
But the bottom line is,
we're going to need a lot of humility.

00:37:18.056 --> 00:37:23.420
We're trying to do something that is new,
we're going to get some things wrong.

00:37:23.420 --> 00:37:26.300
We need to be able to
monitor how well we're doing,

00:37:26.300 --> 00:37:31.510
to learn from our mistakes as well as
our successes, and to adapt as we go.

00:37:32.620 --> 00:37:39.130
So with that I want to just end by saying
that we in our program here at Stanford,

00:37:39.130 --> 00:37:43.290
and believe that there are three really
important characteristics of leaders for

00:37:43.290 --> 00:37:49.570
sustainability, leaders in businesses,
NGOs, academic institutions, governments.

00:37:49.570 --> 00:37:53.160
First of all the ability to
understand these complex systems

00:37:53.160 --> 00:37:55.290
which we've just been talking about.

00:37:55.290 --> 00:37:59.580
And to make decisions recognizing
those five capital assets.

00:37:59.580 --> 00:38:04.669
The second is the mindset and the capacity
to lead in this very dynamic world.

00:38:04.669 --> 00:38:08.619
And Julia Novy-Hildesley,
a Professor of Practice here,

00:38:08.619 --> 00:38:12.420
will be speaking on this topic
later in May, at a webinar.

00:38:13.790 --> 00:38:17.980
And finally, the ability to design
innovations that can scale,

00:38:17.980 --> 00:38:22.040
that can lead to change
at a scale that matters.

00:38:22.040 --> 00:38:24.130
And Professor of Practice, Banny Banerjee,

00:38:24.130 --> 00:38:27.650
we'll be getting a webinar on
that topic also later in May.

00:38:28.670 --> 00:38:31.090
So with that,
I thank you very much for listening,

00:38:31.090 --> 00:38:35.020
for being part of this discussion,
and I look forward to your questions.

00:38:35.020 --> 00:38:35.520
Thank you.

00:38:36.710 --> 00:38:38.010
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you very much, Pamela.

00:38:38.010 --> 00:38:40.990
This was a fascinating conversation.

00:38:40.990 --> 00:38:44.710
And so, with that I'm going to turn it
back over to Pam with a few questions

00:38:44.710 --> 00:38:45.710
that have come up.

00:38:45.710 --> 00:38:49.810
And so, Pam, the first question I
would like to ask you is we talked

00:38:49.810 --> 00:38:54.440
about all these very complex
systems of all these activities.

00:38:54.440 --> 00:38:58.471
Which industry do you think is
the most meaningful to intervene in,

00:38:58.471 --> 00:39:02.710
is it most relevant to, the public sector,
the private sector etc.,

00:39:02.710 --> 00:39:05.153
what are your thoughts on that?

00:39:05.153 --> 00:39:09.168
&gt;&gt; I think I have to say all of the above.

00:39:09.168 --> 00:39:13.843
I think there is a role for everyone when
I teach these courses at Stanford to

00:39:13.843 --> 00:39:16.380
the up and coming leaders in the future.

00:39:16.380 --> 00:39:21.487
I'm basically saying it doesn't matter
where you're going in your life,

00:39:21.487 --> 00:39:26.273
you have an important role to play in
this and whether you're going into

00:39:26.273 --> 00:39:31.167
non-profits or government or
corporations of all kinds, it matters.

00:39:31.167 --> 00:39:35.074
I think, as we look at some
of the big challenges today,

00:39:35.074 --> 00:39:40.720
meeting the energy needs of people,
meeting the food needs of people are huge.

00:39:40.720 --> 00:39:45.490
And all of the industries and
corporations and

00:39:45.490 --> 00:39:49.258
companies engaged in doing those
things have a role to play.

00:39:49.258 --> 00:39:55.645
I think ultimately that example that I've
showed of the Palm Oil Certification

00:39:55.645 --> 00:40:01.840
program, it's maybe the way of the future,
I think more and more.

00:40:01.840 --> 00:40:06.960
Corporations and industries are going to
be working together to try to find even

00:40:06.960 --> 00:40:10.150
level playing fields in
which they can do good for

00:40:10.150 --> 00:40:12.860
themselves as well as do good for
the world.

00:40:12.860 --> 00:40:18.964
There's no doubt that we're going to need
a lot more food, energy, water resources.

00:40:18.964 --> 00:40:22.942
And we're going to have to find
ways to provide them, much,

00:40:22.942 --> 00:40:27.093
much more efficiently in order
to sustain for the long term.

00:40:27.093 --> 00:40:27.625
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:40:27.625 --> 00:40:31.999
So another thread of questions
that I'm seeing come up is around

00:40:31.999 --> 00:40:35.725
the different types of intervention,
or education or

00:40:35.725 --> 00:40:40.998
activity may be needed in first world
countries or more affluent societies.

00:40:40.998 --> 00:40:44.210
And maybe third world or
less affluent societies.

00:40:44.210 --> 00:40:48.690
How are, how are the needs in this two
area different I'm sure both need some

00:40:48.690 --> 00:40:51.288
kind of intervention and, and education.

00:40:51.288 --> 00:40:55.541
&gt;&gt; Right, well it's an interesting
question because I think,

00:40:55.541 --> 00:41:00.061
obviously, in the third world countries,
well, let me start.

00:41:00.061 --> 00:41:02.211
In the most developed
countries of the world,

00:41:02.211 --> 00:41:04.590
we still have lots of
sustainability challenges.

00:41:04.590 --> 00:41:10.434
We have an opportunity to use our
resources, to meet the needs of people,

00:41:10.434 --> 00:41:15.639
to focus on human wellbeing in much
better ways than we are today.

00:41:15.639 --> 00:41:18.180
So it's not like we've got it figured out.

00:41:18.180 --> 00:41:22.325
But I think what's really interesting is
to see the leap frogging in the third

00:41:22.325 --> 00:41:25.091
world, in the developing
countries of the world.

00:41:25.091 --> 00:41:29.983
Where companies and
corporations are moving in to help

00:41:29.983 --> 00:41:35.300
the citizens of those countries,
those nations.

00:41:35.300 --> 00:41:39.962
Meet their needs through better
healthcare, through more food,

00:41:39.962 --> 00:41:44.467
through better water resources
in ways that are conservative.

00:41:44.467 --> 00:41:48.646
In ways that are smart from
a sustainability perspective,

00:41:48.646 --> 00:41:52.586
that are efficient but
still meet the people's needs.

00:41:52.586 --> 00:41:58.683
And I think companies that are engaging
right now in the developing world,

00:41:58.683 --> 00:42:03.037
in some of the poorest parts
of the developing world,

00:42:03.037 --> 00:42:05.680
are among the most innovative.

00:42:05.680 --> 00:42:10.546
Because they have an opportunity
to innovate new approaches that

00:42:10.546 --> 00:42:12.360
actually make sense.

00:42:12.360 --> 00:42:15.997
Both for those people right there and
for the environment and for

00:42:15.997 --> 00:42:19.572
the abilities for future generations
to have their needs met.

00:42:19.572 --> 00:42:25.273
So very exciting and I think that's
the part that excites me most.

00:42:25.273 --> 00:42:30.075
&gt;&gt; So we've talked about the fact that
companies typically measure their success

00:42:30.075 --> 00:42:31.300
in economic terms.

00:42:31.300 --> 00:42:35.320
And early on in the webinar we talked
about the three legs of that stool.

00:42:35.320 --> 00:42:40.054
What are some examples you can give to
ways companies have successfully used,

00:42:40.054 --> 00:42:44.284
what measures have they used to tap
into these other components beyond

00:42:44.284 --> 00:42:45.717
the economic value?

00:42:45.717 --> 00:42:50.154
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean, I think there's some
really wonderful case studies out there.

00:42:50.154 --> 00:42:54.529
I think,
if you take a look at Unilever's 2020

00:42:54.529 --> 00:42:59.620
sustainable living program,
there's a wonderful list of

00:42:59.620 --> 00:43:04.836
ways at every scale in that
corporation they're engaging.

00:43:04.836 --> 00:43:07.624
From the product design stage,

00:43:07.624 --> 00:43:13.509
where they're thinking very
carefully about reducing plastics,

00:43:13.509 --> 00:43:18.671
for example, or
creating new products that require less,

00:43:18.671 --> 00:43:22.820
new detergents that
require less use of water.

00:43:22.820 --> 00:43:27.360
Those kinds of innovations
that then will go out and

00:43:27.360 --> 00:43:30.990
be actually making a big difference
to people all around the world.

00:43:30.990 --> 00:43:34.760
But then you have on the other hand,

00:43:34.760 --> 00:43:38.860
companies that are trying very hard
to manage their supply chains so

00:43:38.860 --> 00:43:43.370
that each part of the supply chain
is actually using best practice.

00:43:43.370 --> 00:43:46.640
And that's incredibly challenging.

00:43:46.640 --> 00:43:52.400
That's where certification programs and
standards become very, very helpful.

00:43:52.400 --> 00:43:56.370
But they're using life
cycle assessment and

00:43:56.370 --> 00:44:01.450
other approaches to try to make sure
that at every stage in the supply chain.

00:44:02.690 --> 00:44:04.280
They're doing the best they can.

00:44:04.280 --> 00:44:07.410
I think that, really,

00:44:07.410 --> 00:44:11.200
sustainability is something that has
to engage every part of a company.

00:44:11.200 --> 00:44:15.610
It can't just be the CEO saying
we should be doing this.

00:44:15.610 --> 00:44:20.860
It has to be the CEO and the C suite
on down, committed to making it happen.

00:44:20.860 --> 00:44:25.737
But giving opportunities to everybody
in the company to use their

00:44:25.737 --> 00:44:28.938
own innovation skills to find solutions.

00:44:28.938 --> 00:44:31.082
And many, many companies, I think,

00:44:31.082 --> 00:44:34.704
have done that with respect to
how they use their own resources.

00:44:34.704 --> 00:44:39.296
They reduce their energy,
they reduce water, they reduce waste and

00:44:39.296 --> 00:44:40.810
they've succeeded.

00:44:40.810 --> 00:44:43.780
They've made money just because of that.

00:44:43.780 --> 00:44:47.996
That's fantastic, but increasingly,
companies are taking that perspective

00:44:47.996 --> 00:44:50.353
outside of the walls of
their own factories.

00:44:50.353 --> 00:44:56.439
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm, all right, so you talked a lot
about the complexity of the system and

00:44:56.439 --> 00:45:02.637
how there are so many different components
to each activity that we want to do.

00:45:02.637 --> 00:45:05.911
Who is ultimately accountable for
that in the sense of,

00:45:05.911 --> 00:45:07.800
since there's so many actors.

00:45:07.800 --> 00:45:13.285
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, I think you have to
put a boundary around your system and

00:45:13.285 --> 00:45:16.150
try to do your best within it.

00:45:16.150 --> 00:45:18.529
Ultimately, nobody is in control.

00:45:18.529 --> 00:45:22.545
We're talking about a global
highly interconnected system.

00:45:22.545 --> 00:45:24.540
And so a lot of this is bottom-up.

00:45:24.540 --> 00:45:30.159
A lot of it is recognizing that
future well-being of one's community,

00:45:30.159 --> 00:45:34.490
one's company,
one's governmental organization or

00:45:34.490 --> 00:45:41.415
non-governmental organization, depends on
everybody doing the best thing they can.

00:45:41.415 --> 00:45:44.317
That's one of the reasons,
I think, the coalitions,

00:45:44.317 --> 00:45:47.592
the partnerships,
that are being developed are so exciting,

00:45:47.592 --> 00:45:51.430
because there is no government for
the globe saying you must do this.

00:45:51.430 --> 00:45:54.180
It has to come from the bottom up.

00:45:54.180 --> 00:45:58.070
That said, the UN's sustainable
development goals that I mentioned at

00:45:58.070 --> 00:46:01.190
the very beginning provide an impetus for
that.

00:46:01.190 --> 00:46:05.873
They've identified really hugely
important areas that need to be

00:46:05.873 --> 00:46:08.224
solved in the developing world.

00:46:08.224 --> 00:46:12.241
And for
companies to think about those goals, and

00:46:12.241 --> 00:46:15.974
to recognize that these
are opportunities for

00:46:15.974 --> 00:46:19.910
them to play in an emerging market,
if you will.

00:46:21.050 --> 00:46:25.760
But to also help solve those problems,
that's the exciting combination.

00:46:25.760 --> 00:46:27.570
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's really wonderful.

00:46:27.570 --> 00:46:32.284
So I think there are a lot of people on
the line today that are realizing that

00:46:32.284 --> 00:46:34.807
this is a very, very important topic.

00:46:34.807 --> 00:46:38.402
And would like to do more to convince
potentially some others within

00:46:38.402 --> 00:46:40.750
the organizer how important this is.

00:46:40.750 --> 00:46:42.130
What are some hooks or

00:46:42.130 --> 00:46:46.470
some strategies that you would use
to help them make that argument?

00:46:46.470 --> 00:46:48.720
&gt;&gt; Yeah, very good question.

00:46:48.720 --> 00:46:53.541
And that's probably one that
the next two speakers in our series

00:46:53.541 --> 00:46:56.753
will be able to address
much better than I,

00:46:56.753 --> 00:47:02.034
because they're more focused on
business settings than I have been.

00:47:02.034 --> 00:47:06.872
But that said, we used to think
about training people to be

00:47:06.872 --> 00:47:11.912
the sustainability director or
the environment director in

00:47:11.912 --> 00:47:17.070
an organization, and
that's still very, very important.

00:47:17.070 --> 00:47:19.400
Those people play very,
very important roles.

00:47:21.040 --> 00:47:23.870
But we also want the top
leaders to get it.

00:47:24.950 --> 00:47:27.707
And one of the big challenges
is how to engage them.

00:47:27.707 --> 00:47:32.358
That's one of the reasons that we're
launching this executive education

00:47:32.358 --> 00:47:36.489
program, because from the very top,
we want people to understand

00:47:36.489 --> 00:47:40.200
both the challenge and
the opportunity for their company.

00:47:40.200 --> 00:47:44.170
I think the companies that have
engaged probably most effectively

00:47:45.180 --> 00:47:50.010
have had both leaders at the bottom and
at the top who've worked together.

00:47:50.010 --> 00:47:55.560
And they've empowered their
employees to be innovative and

00:47:55.560 --> 00:47:59.360
to engage at every stage,
at every part of the organization, so

00:47:59.360 --> 00:48:01.530
it becomes an organization wide effort.

00:48:03.100 --> 00:48:05.720
And I think the payoff is pretty great for
those.

00:48:05.720 --> 00:48:10.152
Both in terms of maintaining,
retaining great employees, but

00:48:10.152 --> 00:48:15.008
also in terms of their own profitability
and social responsibility.

00:48:15.008 --> 00:48:19.296
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I think one thing that might
be embedded in what you said, which we can

00:48:19.296 --> 00:48:23.264
call it more explicitly, is really
talking about both the challenges and

00:48:23.264 --> 00:48:26.336
the opportunities when you
hear of all these different,

00:48:26.336 --> 00:48:29.590
the growing population and
the growth in consumption.

00:48:29.590 --> 00:48:33.319
It's very easy to think about the
challenges and be a little demoralized,

00:48:33.319 --> 00:48:36.751
but I think there's also a lot of
opportunity that could be called out

00:48:36.751 --> 00:48:38.021
as a result of that.

00:48:38.021 --> 00:48:40.666
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, and
I do believe that there are many,

00:48:40.666 --> 00:48:44.160
many corporations in the world
that see the opportunity.

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:48.630
But, I'm glad that many of them
see the opportunity not just

00:48:49.640 --> 00:48:53.250
to have a much larger consumer base.

00:48:53.250 --> 00:48:55.903
But to do it right with
that consumer base so

00:48:55.903 --> 00:48:59.897
that there's a long term benefit
as well as a short term profit.

00:48:59.897 --> 00:49:00.760
&gt;&gt; Well, good.

00:49:00.760 --> 00:49:03.030
So we're nearing the top of the hour.

00:49:03.030 --> 00:49:07.653
I think this webinar potentially and
hopefully has left all of you on

00:49:07.653 --> 00:49:12.129
the line with an appetite and
curiosity to learn more about this.

00:49:12.129 --> 00:49:17.106
And to dive deeper into some of
the ways that we can actually affect

00:49:17.106 --> 00:49:21.993
change as a result of all of
the complex challenges that we face as

00:49:21.993 --> 00:49:25.190
a species really, and as a planet.

00:49:25.190 --> 00:49:27.940
And I think there will be different
opportunities to do that.

00:49:27.940 --> 00:49:29.704
So as Pamela mentioned,

00:49:29.704 --> 00:49:34.492
there are two webinars that will be
coming up in May that will be led by

00:49:34.492 --> 00:49:39.036
Professor Julia Novy-Hildesley as
well as Professor Banny Banerjee.

00:49:39.036 --> 00:49:41.188
Both of these will be talking and

00:49:41.188 --> 00:49:46.458
touching in more depth on some of these
topics that have been discussed today.

00:49:46.458 --> 00:49:51.182
In addition, of course, we invite you to
join us in September to learn more about

00:49:51.182 --> 00:49:52.991
this in person here on campus.

00:49:52.991 --> 00:49:57.334
As a reminder the recording of this
webinar will be emailed to all of you

00:49:57.334 --> 00:49:58.300
within a week.

00:49:58.300 --> 00:50:02.110
So that you can review it again and
share with colleagues and friends.

00:50:02.110 --> 00:50:05.130
And we will also be sending you some
additional information about other

00:50:05.130 --> 00:50:07.160
opportunities to engage with us.

00:50:07.160 --> 00:50:11.133
With that I want to wish you a very good
day and thank you for joining us today.

